### 1.2 Engine — new file `src/lib/wordGenerator.ts` (pure functions, letter-frequency matching)

Implement these functions with EXACTLY this behaviour (write the code fresh in this codebase's style — the signatures and rules below are the specification):

- `normalizeLetters(input: string): string` — uppercase the input, strip spaces and any non A–Z characters (the UI must still validate and show a clear message for numbers/symbols rather than silently dropping them — see `validateWordInput`).
- `countLetterFrequency(text: string): Map<string, number>` — counts every letter in the normalized text.
- `canBuildWord(word: string, availableLetters: Map<string, number>): boolean` — true only if every letter count the word needs is available; a letter may be used no more times than it appears in the input (e.g. APPLE has two P letters, so a word needing three P letters is rejected).
- `findPossibleWords(letters: string): string[]` — every dictionary word buildable from the letters (any length from 2 up to the input length, subject to the active filters).
- `findExactAnagrams(letters: string): string[]` — words whose complete letter-frequency map matches the input exactly (every supplied letter used, no letter omitted, no letter added, repeated letters handled correctly).
- `filterWordsByLength(words: string[], min: number, max: number): string[]` — keeps words within the min/max length (inclusive).
- `sortWords(words: string[], sortMode: 'alphabetical' | 'length-desc' | 'length-asc'): string[]` — alphabetical; longest first (ties alphabetical); shortest first (ties alphabetical).
- `validateWordInput(input: string): { valid: boolean; error: string | null }` — rejects empty input, input containing numbers or symbols, and input longer than the documented maximum (see §1.4 — set the limit, e.g. 15 letters, and state it on the page). Accepts uppercase and lowercase (normalize to uppercase) and optional spaces between letters.
- **Performance:** the engine must scan the dictionary efficiently (frequency maps, not per-word string searches). Cache the loaded dictionary in memory after the first load. Never load the dictionary into the initial page bundle — see §1.3.

